Pi0+A3 (learned SDF) & LoRA + SDF decoder w/ gradient refinement & ${\sim}$34.2M \\
Pi0.5 LoRA & LoRA fine-tune on Pi0.5 & ${\sim}$33.5M \\
Pi0.5+A1 (SDF aux) & LoRA + SDF aux on Pi0.5, $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$ & ${\sim}$34.2M \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Standard Metrics}\label{sec:standard_metrics}

We report two conventional metrics. The \emph{Collision Avoidance Rate} (CAR) is the fraction of episodes in which no collision occurs between the robot and any non-target object:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:car}
\text{CAR} = \frac{|\{e : \text{safe}(e)\}|}{|E|}
\end{equation}
where $E$ is the set of all evaluation episodes and $\text{safe}(e)$ indicates that episode $e$ contains zero collision events. The \emph{Task Success Rate} (TSR) is the fraction of episodes where the manipulation task reaches completion:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:tsr}
\text{TSR} = \frac{|\{e : \text{success}(e)\}|}{|E|}
\end{equation}
These two metrics are standard in the SafeLIBERO evaluation protocol and in the broader VLA safety literature~\cite{hu2025vlsa,zhang2025safevla}.

\subsection{Displacement-Filtered CAR}\label{sec:dfcar}

Standard CAR conflates two qualitatively distinct behaviors: a robot that plans a trajectory around obstacles, and a robot that has collapsed into inaction. Both register as ``safe,'' yet only the former reflects collision avoidance capability. We introduce CAR$_\text{f}$, which excludes action-collapsed episodes from both numerator and denominator.

\paragraph{Action collapse.} We define an episode $e$ as \emph{action-collapsed} when its maximum end-effector (EE) displacement satisfies
\begin{equation}
d_{\max}(e) < \tau_d
\end{equation}
where $d_{\max}(e) = \max_{t} \| \mathbf{p}_t - \mathbf{p}_0 \|_2$ is the maximum Euclidean displacement of the EE from its initial position across all timesteps, and $\tau_d$ is a displacement threshold. We set $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm, corresponding to sub-actuator-resolution motion.

\paragraph{Definition.} The displacement-filtered CAR is:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:car_f}
\text{CAR}_\text{f} = \frac{|\{e : \text{safe}(e) \;\land\; d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}{|\{e : d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}
\end{equation}
CAR$_\text{f}$ retains only episodes with sufficient EE motion and measures the collision avoidance rate exclusively among them.

\paragraph{Threshold robustness.} The specific choice of $\tau_d$ is not critical. The displacement distribution across all evaluated models is sharply bimodal: collapsed episodes cluster below 1\,$\mu$m, while active episodes exceed 200\,mm. No episodes fall in the intermediate range. This five-order-of-magnitude gap means that any $\tau_d \in [2, 200]$\,mm produces identical filtering results. We verify this empirically in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_threshold}.

\paragraph{Diagnostic capability.} CAR$_\text{f}$ serves two functions. First, it detects fake safety: Pi0+A1 reports CAR\,=\,91.5\% under the standard metric, but CAR$_\text{f} \approx 0$\% because 99.6\% of its safe episodes are action-collapsed. Second, it validates genuine safety: Pi0.5 achieves CAR$_\text{f}$ equal to its raw CAR (28.5\%), with 0\% of episodes filtered, because all safe episodes involve EE displacement above 288\,mm.

\subsection{SDF Auxiliary Training}\label{sec:sdf_aux}

We augment the LoRA-adapted VLA with two lightweight auxiliary heads that provide geometric supervision during training.

\paragraph{SDF Decoder ($f_\text{SDF}$).} A 4-layer MLP (hidden dimension 256, Softplus activation with $\beta = 5$, ${\sim}$395K parameters) predicts the signed distance $\Phi(\mathbf{x})$ from the current EE position $\mathbf{x}$ to the nearest obstacle surface. The decoder reads from intermediate features of the VLA action expert. The ground-truth signed distance field is precomputed from obstacle meshes using trimesh/pysdf, with values clamped at $\delta = 0.05$\,m and a safety margin $m = 0.01$\,m applied to shift the zero-crossing outward from the obstacle surface.

\paragraph{EE Extractor ($f_\text{EE}$).} A 2-layer MLP ($1024 \to 256 \to 3$, ${\sim}$263K parameters) predicts the 3D EE position from the same action expert features. This auxiliary head provides the query point for the SDF decoder and serves as an implicit regularizer on the learned feature representation.

\paragraph{Training objective.} The total loss combines the standard flow-matching action loss with the two auxiliary losses:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:total_loss}
\mathcal{L}_\text{total} = \mathcal{L}_\text{action} + \lambda \left( \mathcal{L}_\text{SDF} + \mathcal{L}_\text{EE} \right)
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}_\text{SDF}$ is a clamped $L_1$ loss on predicted SDF values with an Eikonal regularizer, $\mathcal{L}_\text{EE}$ is MSE on predicted EE position, and $\lambda$ controls the auxiliary loss weight. We evaluate $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$. The total auxiliary overhead is $<$660K parameters, less than 2\% of the ${\sim}$33.5M LoRA parameter budget.

\paragraph{Optimization.} All models are trained with AdamW, a cosine learning rate schedule decaying from $2.5 \times 10^{-5}$ to $2.5 \times 10^{-6}$, for 30K steps with batch size 32. For Pi0.5+A1, training proceeds in two phases: Phase~1 (steps 0--5K) trains only the auxiliary heads on detached features with the backbone frozen; Phase~2 (steps 5K--30K) unfreezes LoRA parameters for joint optimization.

\subsection{Oracle SDF Refinement}\label{sec:oracle_setup}

To test the theoretical upper bound of EE-only SDF refinement (Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}), we replace the learned SDF network with the ground-truth signed distance field computed from the simulation geometry. This eliminates prediction error and isolates the structural limitation of the refinement paradigm.

We evaluate three oracle variants:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Oracle-adaptive}: runtime gradient refinement with a dynamic step size $\alpha$ that scales inversely with gradient magnitude, applied in Cartesian action space.
\item \textit{Oracle-fixed}: a constant step size $\alpha = 0.005$, also in Cartesian action space.
\item \textit{Oracle-feature}: gradient applied in the VLA's latent feature space rather than directly to Cartesian actions. This variant averages 241.7 refinement steps per episode (vs.\ ${\sim}$14 for the Cartesian variants), as the feature-space gradient must propagate through the action decoder.
\end{itemize}
Each variant is evaluated for 160 episodes (4 tasks $\times$ 40 episodes) with a single seed. The oracle experiments are designed to test Claim~2 (Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}): if EE-only SDF refinement is structurally sound, oracle performance should exceed unrefined baselines by a wide margin.

